An unordered, forward-facing backlog; completed work and its rationale
live in the git history of this file, docs/plans/, docs/design/, and
NEWS.

Items are listed alphabetically by plan name; the order carries no
meaning. Most items have an implementation plan in docs/plans/<name>.md
(a few record instead in a design doc or a differently-named plan,
named in the item), and the planning/implementation/review process (who
plans, who implements, who reviews, which gates run) is
docs/plans/README.md.
Gate labels (decision-gated, consumer-gated) rank priority rather than
block work (VD 2026-07-17); where a later VD stamp schedules an item
after 1.0-0, the item carries that stamp.
Standing rule (VD 2026-08-06): gate an item IF we cannot think of
anything valuable it might enable - that failure, not the absence of
a caller today, is the only licensed gating fact. "consumer-gated"
below reads accordingly, and the gate lifts the moment a valuable
enabled model or use comes to mind. An existing consumer is
sufficient evidence of value, never necessary.

Standing fact (VD): large datasets are common, and multiple chains
remain the default. The "single-chain n >= 1e5" spelling this entry
used to carry is an agent paraphrase VD disowns (docs/decisions.md
dec-B50, VD 2026-09-08). The large-n performance items are live, not
consumer-gated.

Standing discipline (from the 2026-08-07 census): verify an entry's
status claims against git and live code before scoping from it;
re-run a full census before release.

A "door" below means a recorded, undecided question, not a task.

items
  approximate-distributions: research arc, post-release (VD 2026-09-29,
    docs/decisions.md dec-B143): whether to admit approximate draws
    under stated criteria for when to use them, against dec-B14's
    every-draw-exact rule. Covers the negative-binomial latent at large
    shape (cost linear in y + r today; the exact construction, 1,000 to
    2,000 lines, is designed in docs/plans/nbinom-large-shape-pg.md and
    not scheduled), real shape (negbin-real-dispersion) and
    non-integer logistic weights (weighted-binary).
  bcf-latent-mixing: research door, no plan doc of its own; for the
    post-merge mixing research. The probit and logistic BCF chains mix
    slowly at large prognostic amplitude, and two gates carry that as a
    standing cost. The latent SBC arms stay out of the sbc.yaml matrix:
    their ladders fail the admission clause at |a| >= 5 on both links,
    with or without the amplitude rescaling move (docs/plans/
    bcf-latent-evidence.md, Decision 1). bcf-latent-exact.R's K = 3
    probit arm keeps the slow-chain statistic, its seed spread up to 7x
    its 400-batch se while unbiased pooled over 300 seeds, and fails
    about 3 quick runs in 100 on a correct sampler; left as is by VD
    2026-09-25 ("Leave it for now, but keep in mind that we have mixing
    work to do down the road and will perhaps want to revisit").
    Revisit both when a mixing change lands. The removed rescaling move
    is one candidate to re-measure there (docs/decisions.md dec-B127;
    restoring it requires the cache rule in docs/design/
    multiplier-combiner.md). research-open.
  bcf-sigma-tail-mixing: research door, no plan doc of its own. In the
    EXTREME tail (|a0| >= ~40, ~3% of the prior) the chain is
    frozen-structure mixing that no amount of burn fixes; a principled
    remedy would target tree-structure mixing at high SNR (tempered
    early sweeps, restart-style grow proposals). Low priority - the
    regime only arises with a stale build scale swapped against a new
    response inside a larger Gibbs loop (setResponse
    updateScale = FALSE). Record:
    docs/plans/archive/bcf-sigma-residual.md sec 4 item 2. The gaussian
    SBC arm at its recorded settings (72000-sweep burn, thin 30, R = 200)
    passes sigma at 0.0602 against a 0.0917 band and flags prog3 and
    prog4 (0.1061, 0.1209) without the amplitude rescaling move
    (2026-09-24); it does not reach the tail regime this door names.
    research-open, decision-gated.
  capi-unwind-protect: closed but for one arm. A host draw callback's R
    error is now caught at the callback itself, where R_UnwindProtect can
    still turn it into a C++ unwind, so the engine's run frames run their
    destructors before the jump resumes; the engine raises into R nowhere
    (the prior draws and the predict fan-out throw, and the bridge and the
    flat entries convert), and the chain rng buffer is released before its
    own raise. What is left is not the engine's: the rng library's
    unimplemented-kind refusals in external/randomBase.c and randomNorm.c
    are C and still raise through R from under a run. Both are default
    arms over kinds the library implements, so nothing reaches them
    today; closing them means an error channel out of the C rng API.
  correlated-outcomes: AR-1 / serially-correlated errors. Longitudinal,
    panel, ITS and DiD compose today via a latent AR-state + nugget in
    the outer sampler, driven through the setOffset conduit; EXACT
    pure-AR-1 errors
    are a deferred ENGINE door - banded residual precision plus
    cross-leaf GLS leaf updates break per-leaf independence. It opens
    on a consumer demanding exact AR-1 errors. Record:
    docs/design/correlated-outcomes.md (A). causal-motivated,
    decision-gated.
  doc-freshness-guard-tightening: door, VD decision. The symbol rule
    in tools/check-doc-freshness.R accepts a cite when each component
    occurs anywhere in the target file, and the guard reads docs/design
    and docs/plans but not man/ or vignettes/ for content drift, so
    prose can go stale while every cite passes. Tightening either half
    (symbol inside the cited construct; content coverage of man and
    vignettes) will surface pre-existing drift needing a content re-pin
    pass before it can go green. No plan doc until ruled.
  equivalence-harness-seeds-axis: post-RC, re-record
    benchmarks/R/bcf-equivalence.R and multinomial-equivalence.R
    against independent per-scenario seeds in place of one chain's
    autocorrelated draws - a |z| bar over 40 correlated draws tolerates
    a shift of ~0.9 posterior sd per cell, which is why the statistical
    tier is decoupled from the gate. Record:
    docs/plans/bcf-cross-host.md sec 2.
  fit-na-action-null: door, no plan doc of its own. At fit time,
    na.action = NULL falls back to na.omit on the formula path (R's
    model.frame) but applies no action on the matrix path, which then errors
    on a missing response. Found while planning predict's na.action.
  gp-followups: sampled lengthscales and low-rank kernels; gp() takes a
    fixed positive numeric lengthscale today. Sampling one needs a
    theta prior, per-sample theta channels in the state and flatten
    formats, and costs multiples of the draw phase per sweep (kernel
    cache invalidation per theta draw). A decision memo comes first
    (docs/plans/gp-followups.md). Value holds: GP-leaf BART as a
    surrogate for sequential design of computer experiments (arXiv
    1203.1078), where a FIXED lengthscale asks the user to know the
    thing they are estimating, and low-rank kernels serve the standing
    n >= 1e5 fact. decision-gated on cost, not on value.
  gpu-bart: the backend-seam design waits on frontier items 4-5, the
    informed-kernel and preconditioned-CG prototypes
    (docs/design/parallel-bart-frontier.md sec 5). Memo and falsifiers:
    docs/design/gpu-bart.md.
  grow-from-root: frontier 3.1's exact informed-proposal use of the
    shared cut scan (docs/design/parallel-bart-frontier.md sec 3.1).
  interaction-constraints: formal heredity (an ensemble lattice prior)
    - a fourth formal beside interactions(max.order, groups, forbid)
    plus a prior change. Value gate lifted: it enables
    functional-ANOVA BART (arXiv 2509.03317), for which dbarts already
    ships the hard-constraint half. Post-release, no fixed position: it
    happens, when is open (VD 2026-09-08, dec-B95). Owed there and not
    yet written: man/interactions.Rd states the two-stage workaround
    meanwhile, which today only the design doc does. Soft path-dependent
    penalties: gate STANDS, no model surfaced. Records:
    docs/design/interaction-constraints.md,
    docs/plans/archive/interaction-constraints.md.
  leaf-prior-predictive: no plan doc of its own; filed by the dec-A106
    ruling with docs/plans/leaf-prior-k-or-sd.md. A helper returning the
    prior spread of f at given x under a sampler's leaf prior, since a
    named sd is the sd of the leaf model's own parameter and is the
    prior sd of f(x) only for the constant leaf (linear: a lower bound,
    gp: an upper bound, monotone: a lower bound in the interior).
    samplePriorPredictive already draws f from the prior at x.test; the
    helper would summarize it, or compute it in closed form where one
    exists (constant, linear).
  pdbart-closed-arguments: at 1.1-0, when pdbart's BayesTree spellings
    are refused, replace pdbart's and pd2bart's ... with bart's usable
    arguments listed explicitly, pinned by a test against bart's formals
    less the excluded set (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B212).
  pdbart-hazard-compiled: a compiled routine for pdbart's survival-scale
    partial dependence on hazard fits, so a fine period grid (the
    default, from the observed event times) runs at sizes the fit
    itself does; the R route's work grows with the square of the
    subjects there and a size check stops it. Scheduled after 1.0-0
    (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B209).
  pdbart-ice: individual conditional expectation curves from pdbart and
    pd2bart - one posterior curve per row beside their average - so
    effect heterogeneity shows with its uncertainty (pdp::partial's
    ice, iml). Memory grows as rows x grid points x draws; a per-row
    posterior mean and interval keeps it bounded. Scheduled after 1.0-0
    (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B207).
  pdbart-per-category: partial dependence per category for multinomial
    and ordinal fits from pdbart and pd2bart - each category's
    probability as the variable moves, a category margin on the result
    and one line per category in the plots. Refused until then.
    Scheduled after 1.0-0 (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B209).
  setmodel-parameters-not-structure: setModel changes a model's
    parameters and refuses a model that differs in structure, by name,
    before anything is stored (VD 2026-10-05, dec-B254). To build, for
    1.0-0, after the merge to main if need be: the refusal of every
    structural difference (today another family's model, a Student-t
    change, interaction limits, blocks and Gaussian-process settings are
    accepted and not honoured); setters for the Student-t degrees of
    freedom, a fixed negative-binomial shape, a Gaussian-process
    lengthscale and tree priors on forests past the first and on the
    variance forest; DART's own settings, and the ordinary prior
    settings on a DART sampler; and five defects: run()'s k column after
    k is switched between fixed and drawn (a regression against 0.9-34),
    a zero split probability on a column the trees use (and, with it,
    that a zero-probability column is still split on once the
    positive ones run out along a path, as in 0.9-34:
    CGMTreePrior::drawSplitVariable returns the first available column
    whatever its probability), a tree-move
    mixture that does not reach the variance forest, raw leaf-prior
    slots edited alone, and a residual prior stored on a family with no
    residual scale.
  warm-start-across-models: a warm start from a donor that is another
    model, such as a fast fit on a transformed response seeding a slower
    model on the original scale, with whatever scale or dispersion the
    two do not share translated. After 1.0-0 (VD 2026-10-05, dec-B248).
  interrupt-gaps: what a user's interrupt still does not reach or
    reaches as an error (docs/plans/interrupt-is-interrupt.md, landing
    note). Not polled at all: $sampleTreesFromPrior,
    $sampleLeafParametersFromPrior, predict and the R-side latent
    refresh. On the sweep-callback entry a real signal or a time limit
    arrives while the closure runs under R's evaluator and comes out as
    "error evaluating the sweep callback": evaluate the closure under a
    handler for interrupts and errors, as the poll does, an interrupt
    cancelling the run and an error keeping its message. The entry has
    no caller in R/ today.
  warm-start-salvage: bart(warm.start = ) takes all it can from a
    donor: a donor tree the recipient cannot hold (a split on a column
    its forest may not use, an order a monotone leaf forbids) is
    collapsed at that split to one leaf and the fit proceeds, where
    today the whole donor is refused; installTrees gains a force
    argument, without which it accepts or refuses the donor whole, as
    built (dec-B295, VD 2026-10-07). Before 1.0-0; designed with
    warm-start-tree-count, the same question for a donor of another
    tree count. Ruled since: a part only one side has, DART's
    weights or a variance forest, does not transfer and the trees do
    (dec-B324); a constant donor seeds linear leaves exactly and a
    linear donor constant ones at the covariates' centre, gp refused
    (dec-B325); bart says what was taken in one verbose line
    (dec-B326); forced forms return NULL (dec-B310). To design: the
    argument's name and default, a collapsed leaf's value, and what
    stays refused under force.
  warm-start-tree-count: a warm start (bart's warm.start, the sampler's
    installTrees) from a donor with another tree count, partially
    populated: a smaller recipient takes some of the donor's trees, a
    larger one takes them all and starts the rest empty. Today a donor
    with another count is refused as not shape-compatible. It is the
    path for what 0.9-34's setControl did when given another n.trees,
    which 1.0-0 refuses (VD 2026-10-05, dec-B247: "maybe we can add only
    partially populating trees from another model in the warm-starts to
    handle the big -> small, small -> big scenarios"). Ruled: a smaller recipient
    takes the donor's trees whose fits vary most over its rows, values
    as they are; a larger one takes them all, the rest single leaves at
    0 (dec-B323).
  warm-start-undo-test: Sampler::installForests undoes a refused warm
    start by snapshotting each touched chain's state and setting it back
    (restoreTouched), and nothing tests it. Every refusal it guards is
    checked before any chain is touched, so the undo runs only on an
    allocation failure or a rebuild that passed its pre-check and then
    failed; neither can be reached from tinytest or tests/cpp without a
    fault-injection hook. Add one (a tests/cpp peer that fails the Nth
    installForest) and check the recipient is restored: trees, sigma, k,
    DART state, transform and the next draws.
  state-frame-prior: the cut grid, a linear or gp leaf's covariate
    standardization with its default lengthscale, and the response
    range are data derived once and then held (dec-B231 to dec-B233):
    they belong on the data object, change only by a call that names
    them, and are never installed by a state. Landed: setState's
    return value (docs/plans/restore-status.md), a set cut grid
    that can be put back (docs/plans/cut-points-undo.md) and the leaf
    conversions (docs/plans/leaf-conversions.md). Not yet planned: the
    three
    records on dbartsData; creation and setData using a carried
    record (setData's updateCutPoints, updateScale and
    updateStandardization; setPredictor's updateStandardization); a
    state install that compares the stored values, moves splits by
    value and converts slopes instead of installing them; the manual's
    table of what puts each value back before setState; the gp
    setData refusal narrowed to the cases that would change a kept
    draw. The design is not in docs/design yet. The grid half is
    before 1.0-0: today setState puts back the cut grid a state was
    stored under, undoing a later setCutPoints (run 2026-10-08: 3
    points set, 100 after the restore, TRUE), against dec-B231 and
    0.9-34, whose state carried no grid; shipped, a caller could rely
    on it.
  k-calibration-ends: the probit-k calibration arm passes its gate
    but put an end bin of k's ranks far over (48 of 30 expected on
    one stream, 52 on another, chi-square p 0.012 and 0.013): draws
    too correlated after thinning, or a k step too narrow. Settle
    which before the merge (dec-B301).
  workflow-text-edits: equivalence.yaml, rchk.yaml and valgrind.yaml
    say workflow_dispatch needs main, which it does not; sbc.yaml's
    aft job has a 15-minute limit that a slow dependency install
    exhausts (cancelled once, 2026-10-08). Edit them together, which
    starts each workflow in full.
  basis-swap-zero-column: setForestBasis takes a numeric basis column
    of all zeros, as it takes a factor level no row holds (dec-B346),
    where today it refuses it; creation still refuses one. Help and a
    test. Before 1.0-0.
  predict-training-rows: predict on a fit with no newdata returns each
    type at the training rows from the stored draws, needing no saved
    trees, an offset given there replacing the fit's (dec-B341), where
    today it stops with "newdata cannot be NULL". Every family's
    predict method, help and tests. Before 1.0-0.
  survival-offset-training-rows: survivalProbabilities given an offset
    and no newdata applies it at the training rows in place of the
    fit's, off the stored draws with no trees replayed (dec-B340),
    where today it is refused. Help and a test. Before 1.0-0.
  binary-one-class-fit: a probit or logistic fit whose response holds
    one class is fitted with one warning saying so, where today it is
    refused (dec-B333, revising dec-A144); tests of the latent sampler
    at one class. Before 1.0-0.
  gp-reanchor-message: the refusal of updateScale = TRUE by a gp
    sampler that holds saved draws says to re-derive the scale during
    burn-in, before draws are saved (burn-in sweeps, or keepTrees off
    until the scale is fixed), or to make a new sampler, in place of
    "a saved gp draw replays only under the response range it was
    drawn with" (dec-B330). Bridge text, test-capi.R and
    test-leaf-conversions.R. Before 1.0-0.
  leaf-slope-formula-single-value: at setData and a warm start a
    linear leaf's live slope is converted by the formula onto and off
    a covariate that holds one value at its centre, where today it is
    set to zero and the leaf keeps its value or takes its function's
    value at the new constant (dec-B329, revising dec-A177); the help
    for newData says so. Before 1.0-0.
  multiforest-scale-rederive: after 1.0-0 (dec-B362, VD 2026-10-08).
    updateScale = TRUE on a sampler of several forests, refused today:
    restate each forest's default size on the new scale, a stated
    forest(sd = ) kept as given, so the burn-in re-derivation practice
    reaches bcf-shaped models. After the forest slices. Before then
    the help says a re-derived basis scale (updateBasisScale, dec-B303)
    and the response scale may span different rows.
  predictor-frame-whole: before 1.0-0 (dec-B359, VD 2026-10-08).
    setPredictor(x) with no column takes a data frame, coded by label
    as the column form is, where today it fails ("'list' object cannot
    be coerced to type 'double'", as in 0.9-34); a numeric matrix is
    taken only where every predictor column is numeric and is refused
    by name on a sampler with a factor column. The same for
    setTestPredictor and predict (dec-B360), which take a matrix of
    codes on a factor fit today (predict(fit, cbind(a = 0.5, f = 2))
    reads 2 as the second level).
  hazard-max-rows-cost: before 1.0-0 (dec-B367, VD 2026-10-08). The
    help of hazard(max.rows = ) and its refusal state what a row costs:
    about 210 bytes a row shared plus about 630 a row a chain at 75
    trees (one stored value a row a tree), so 1e7 rows is about 8.5 GB
    on one chain and 27 GB on bart()'s four. The default stays 1e7.
  hazard-period-every-forest: before 1.0-0 (dec-B365, VD 2026-10-08).
    Every forest of a hazard fit may split on its period column
    whatever its vars, as a single forest already may; today each of
    several forests (dbarts(forests = list(...))) takes vars literally
    and splits on period only where it is named. The test in
    test-single-forest-vars.R that pins the literal reading changes.
    A forest constant over time has no spelling in 1.0-0.
  leaf-covariate-cap-removed: before 1.0-0 (dec-B366, VD 2026-10-08).
    Linear and gp leaves take any number of covariates: the per-leaf
    scratch (LinearGaussianLeaf::maxNumCovariates,
    maxFunctionLeafCovariates in tree.hpp) is sized at creation in
    place of a stack array of 8; the R refusal goes, and the limits
    table in dbartsControl.Rd loses its row. No draw changes at 8 or
    fewer; a speed check at small q against the stack form.
  perturb-width-control: before 1.0-0 (dec-B366, VD 2026-10-08). The
    perturb move's window (perturbWidth in moves.hpp, 1 grid position)
    becomes a dbartsControl setting beside proposal.probs, a whole
    number of grid positions, default 1, which changes no draw; the
    limits table's row says it is settable.
  leaf-prior-spelling-keeps-spread: before 1.0-0 (dec-B356, VD
    2026-10-08). setLeafPrior switching a drawn leaf prior between
    normal(k = ) and normal(sd = invchi()) keeps each chain's spread
    in force, writing k_old x k.scale_new / k.scale_old, where today
    it keeps k and the spread jumps; setLeafPrior's help and the R
    docstring change with it. Open for VD: whether a changed invchi()
    scale within the sd spelling, the same mechanism, keeps the spread
    too.
  leaf-covariate-single-value-off-centre: a leaf covariate constant
    to the eye but not to the bit keeps a rounding-sized scale: a floor
    relative to the centre closes it. A slope on a column moved to
    another single value stays converted by the formula (dec-B328).
    With the slice that takes the centre and scale from the rows in
    the likelihood (dec-B304), which moves the same test (dec-A177).
  missing-label-refusal-text: setPredictor and the joint row update
    refuse a missing label for a factor column with "has missing
    values, which its training values do not"; on a column of 70
    levels whose training values had two missing the same words are
    given, which is false there. Find whether only the words are
    wrong or the refusal too (docs/plans/small-rulings-batch.md,
    landing note).
  cut-point-weights: a way to make some cut points likelier than
    others: an argument of probabilities beside the grid,
    setCutPoints(cuts, column, probs = ), as split.probs weights
    columns; never by repeating a point, no grid repeating one in any
    version (dec-B300, dec-B297). After 1.0-0. The engine picks
    uniformly among grid positions today, in the prior and the
    proposal both.
  sigma-prior-setter: a setter for the residual prior in
    setLeafPrior's idiom, setSigmaPrior(chisq(df, scale)), and chisq()
    taking scale beside quant, the two not given together (dec-B314,
    VD 2026-10-07). sigest stays an aid at creation and no call
    re-estimates it. Design first: the unit of scale (an sd in the
    response's units, as a forest's sd is, or a variance), whether
    sigest beside a stated scale is refused, what the setter does to a
    live chain and after burn-in, and the argument VD allowed that
    says whether a scale is absolute or relative to the response's
    scale, a relative one following updateScale = TRUE. With or after
    the slice of response-scale-rows that adds the re-derivation
    arguments.
  hazard-periods-rows-in: a hazard fit's default periods are the
    distinct observed times of the subjects in the likelihood at
    creation, where today a subject at weight 0 gives its time too
    and adds rows to the subjects in (dec-B315, VD 2026-10-07). A
    subject at weight 0 is expanded on that grid, its time in the
    interval it falls in, one past the last period at risk throughout
    with no event. R only; with the creation slice of
    response-scale-rows, whose rule it follows.
  default-rule-per-gap: BEFORE THE MERGE TO MAIN (dec-B313, VD
    2026-10-07). Under the default rule a column with fewer distinct
    values than n.cuts gets one cut point in each gap between them, as
    the quantile rule gives it, where today it gets n.cuts evenly
    spaced points whatever its values (100 on a 0/1 column, all making
    one split). Changes every default fit with a binary or few-valued
    numeric column and moves recorded baselines. Plan first; a slice
    of its own, in the routine that derives a grid.
  level-shift-capi: two flat C API entries so a host can move a constant
    between the forest and its own intercepts - a read of the occupied
    leaves' count and value sum (original response units) and an apply
    that adds c/m to every occupied leaf of every tree and c to the
    cached totals. stan4bart needs it for the shared level of its group
    intercepts, which is confounded with the forest's level and mixes at
    lag-one 0.97 to 0.99 (stan4bart docs/plans/re-scale-mixing.md); the
    host's draw is conjugate Gaussian, Jacobian one. The engine already
    performs the move internally (Chain::drawLevelShift, the
    multinomial combiner's afterCombine); constant leaf only, refused
    under a stale partition or a constrained leaf, empty leaves skipped.
    About 360 lines with tests. Post-1.0 minor append: the header is
    built for it and stan4bart gates on major equality plus a minor
    floor, so it does not precede the freeze. Do it after stan4bart's
    ridge move lands, when the shared level is the remaining failure.
  manual-gate-runs: benchmarks/R/composition-matrix.R, an executable
    oracle against docs/design/feature-matrix.md, runs in no workflow -
    run it once at the release candidate. Growing sbc.yaml past its
    6-of-26 which subset waits until it has run once on main. Both stay
    manual by decision (docs/plans/pre-review-cleanup.md s5).
  memory-saved-tree-arena: no plan doc of its own; post-release (dec-A33,
    docs/plans/memory-footprint-audit.md's ranked list). Saved trees are
    one vector of nodes per tree, per forest and per saved sample, so each
    carries about 40 bytes of vector object and allocator header on top
    of its nodes; a flat arena per chain, indexed by saved sample, forest
    and tree, drops that overhead and the allocation count. The audit put
    the saving at about 4 MB per chain at S*T = 1e5 saved trees, growing
    with S*T, and nothing at keepTrees = FALSE, so it is small beside the
    other items on that list and waits behind them. One engine struct;
    the flat tree layout that storeState and getTrees already write is the
    shape to reuse.
  memory-wall-frontier: the live perf frontier. The fused pass's
    unmeasured legs - weighted, fp32 residual, move-phase child
    gathers, vector/function leaves, BCF/multinomial, monotone,
    growForestFromRoot, K = 8 - are enumerated at
    docs/design/memory-wall-frontier.md sec 11; an arm A/B on both
    architectures is the confirmation to demand before any arc lands,
    since the arm64 no-signal case inverts the Zen2 win.
  monotone-barker-hybrid: post-release (VD 2026-10-01, dec-B153): the
    count-free Barker move for large "leaf" moves
    (docs/design/monotone-barker-hybrid.md), with stage 4b's order-count
    cache (dec-A134). Only "leaf" fits of one to five trees are slow today;
    they keep the slow-count warning and interrupt. What the cache
    saves is to be measured when it is built (VD 2026-10-07,
    dec-A134). The maintainer noted
    (2026-10-01, dec-B168) that slow-count reporting through the flat C
    run is an argument for it.
  monotone-structural-move: a tree move beyond birth and death that
    respects a monotone constraint, changing a split's rule while the
    leaves stay in order, so that fits of very few trees mix
    (dec-A133). Research, after 1.0-0.
  monotone-exact-birth-death: before 1.0-0 (VD 2026-09-29,
    docs/decisions.md dec-B144): the monotone birth/death move targets the
    documented prior exactly - the whole tree's cone normalizer ratio in
    place of the conditional cone masses - with an exact gate over
    multi-split trees and the monotone SBC arm as verification. Design
    first; the normalizer counts the leaf orderings the constraints admit,
    and the design bounds its cost with several constrained predictors.
    Planned in docs/plans/monotone-exact-birth-death.md (dec-B145..B153);
    stages 1 (redraw, empty leaves, reachability), 2 (the monotone()
    constructor and vocabulary) and 3 (the counter, not wired in) landed;
    3b (leaf geometry on factor and missing-value axes), 4 (the exact move
    under both priors) and 5 (slow counts, cancel, setModel refusals)
    landed; the checkpoint fired its trigger (the hybrid is
    monotone-barker-hybrid below).
    Stage 6 landed: the equivalence scenarios, the successive-conditional
    check, the SBC arms (both 20-tree arms pass), the feel study, the
    default flip to "joint" (dec-B151; cgm()'s tree prior, mBART's values
    an opt-in, dec-B152) and step 12's docs. Open: the Verification's
    quiet-machine speed check (monotone sweep time at 20 trees under each
    prior within 5% of the pre-plan engine, and bench-sampler compare on
    the unconstrained paths).
  monotone-slow-count-test-intermittent: tests/cpp's "monotone slow
    counts: a lowered threshold tallies counts and sizes" failed once in
    sixteen runs of an unchanged test_monotone.cpp. The check asks that
    the slowest count seen had at least two leaves; which count is
    slowest is decided by wall time, so a stump's count can win. Unproven
    reading - reproduce, then pin the tally on something the clock does
    not choose.
  gp-kept-draw-kernel (dec-B237, after 1.0-0): a kept draw from a gp leaf
    stores the centre, scale and lengthscale it was drawn under and
    replays under them, so a gp-leaf sampler holding kept draws can take a
    change of standardization (setData deriving one, or
    updateStandardization = TRUE) and a state whose kept gp draws were
    made under another, which 1.0-0 refuses. Conditional: only a gp
    sampler that keeps draws carries the record. About 350 lines: the
    ring store and its resize, the writer, the one replay routine every
    predict path reaches, the state writer and reader. Then restate or
    drop the refusal of kept gp draws under another named lengthscale
    (dec-A146), whose reason this removes.
    The refusals are step 5 of docs/plans/leaf-conversions.md. Of
    them dec-B237 rules the standardization only; refusing a
    re-derived response range is the coordinator's decision of
    2026-10-06, recorded in that plan's calls made in planning and
    not yet marked. The kernel record lifts the standardization half
    of the setData refusal; the range half and the updateScale = TRUE
    refusals stand until a kept gp draw also records the response
    range it was drawn under.
  classic-compare-quantile-row: the compare against 0.9-34 has one
    quantile scenario, 500 distinct values at 20 cuts, and since the
    quantile split points were spread over the whole column (dec-B235)
    it separates on the grid alone, so no row of the release battery
    measures the engine under useQuantiles. Add a scenario whose columns
    have no more distinct values than cuts plus one, where the old and
    new grids are the same, and take its 0.9-34 side.
  quantile-refresh-held-count: a quantile column keeps the cut count it
    was created with at every refresh (setPredictor with
    updateCutPoints = TRUE), so a column created with few distinct
    values, a latent that starts constant for one, never gains split
    points however many values it later takes. Raise the count toward
    n.cuts at a refresh; trees hold split indices, so it needs the
    by-value move a data replacement uses.
  partial-update-missing-gauge: after per-observation updates
    (forceUpdate = "partial") fill every missing value of a column, the
    live sampler still treats the column as holding missing values and
    keeps its rules' missing directions, while copy() of the same state
    drops them (28 rules live, 7 after the copy in a probe). Re-derive
    the column's missing flag when a partial update removes its last
    missing value.
  pooled-missing-direction-drop: after a column's missing values are
    filled, a pooled categorical rule keeps its missing-direction bit (the
    forced update and a state install both leave it), so swap and change
    treat the subtree holding it as invalid and skip it until the rule is
    pruned or redrawn: 19 such rules after an install, 1 after 50 sweeps,
    none after 250. Clearing the bit in the shared drop makes the tree
    valid at once and changes draws after a pooled fill, so it is a
    shifting slice with its own gates.
  monotone-factor-direction-host: a host that hands the engine a monotone
    direction on an unordered-factor column gets an unconstrained sampler
    without a word: the facade's monotoneConstraintIsActive returns false
    and the whole constraint is dropped. R refuses such a direction before
    it reaches the engine; the flat C entry points should refuse it too.
  monotone-count-alloc-message: only the move's normalizer ratio turns an
    allocation failure into the message naming the remedies; building
    the order and the exact prior leaf draw still surface a bare
    "std::bad_alloc", and the run-level catch that makes any allocation
    failure an R error also covers failures outside a count, where the
    leaf store may be half-written when the fits are re-summed.
  multiforest-mutation-gaps: two doors
    (docs/plans/archive/multiforest-mutation-gaps.md).
    BCF whole-data setData (n free, z required): KEEP UNDESIGNED,
    gated on a considered failure to find an enabled model - every
