  tree-mixing-proposals: research door - how is the BART posterior
    sticky, and what proposal mechanisms beyond the shipped four-move
    set would help? The move census ran; the same-variable cut redraw
    "perturb" is in the kernel at default weight zero (ab49f83a) with
    its detailed-balance gate (30472110). Its benefit stage (slice 3)
    has run and killed the move at the pre-registered w = 1, d = 0.16
    (d73fb4e0): the kernel stays at default weight zero as an opt-in
    with nil measured benefit, closing slice 4's nonzero default share;
    it stays through 1.0-0 by maintainer ruling 2026-09-14
    (docs/decisions.md dec-A47), revisited by the mixing research. The
    nog-node rule Gibbs draw "rule_gibbs" is in the kernel
    at default weight zero too (7fb166ca) and its benefit stage has run
    (50032833): it is the first kernel change to move C1's summed
    minimum ESS, +22 over twenty matched pairs at two seed blocks
    against a +8 bar and at 2.21 cut-scan sweep-equivalents, so its kill
    does not fire; its coverage regression against the control was a
    reference-arm artifact, dissolved by the 2026-09-08 re-measurement;
    the rule draw's default weight stays zero until the mixing research
    sets one (dec-B96, below). The starting forest's law, an exact
    rejection draw from the restricted prior, is revisited here too
    (dec-A10, VD 2026-09-26).
    Joint structure-and-value proposals (VD 2026-09-27, dec-A71): the
    non-conjugate move strategy of docs/design/gp-leaves.md part 2 -
    birth drawing child values from the prior or a Laplace approximation,
    accepted on the likelihood ratio - is on this list as a candidate
    mixing gain for every leaf model, not GP leaves alone; the second
    sweep variant it needs is the largest piece.
    xbart warm starts (VD 2026-09-28, dec-A05): a fresh chain per fold
    pays a full burn-in; a partial carry-over from a neighbouring cell
    or fold, such as keeping the stumps or half the trees, is a
    candidate once the mixing research says what burn-in buys.
    A per-draw joint log-posterior (Stan's lp__ analogue: tree-structure
    prior, leaf prior, sigma prior and likelihood) is a candidate mixing
    diagnostic for this research; not an extract type for 1.0-0, and not
    for loo, which takes the pointwise log-likelihood (VD 2026-09-28
    asked).
    Owed there: the equal-cost arm on a quiet machine (slice 2's
    rule-gibbs-balance.R has landed). Rotation, a Stage R0
    generator-only regrow census, the re-specified reallocation census
    and heated companion chains stay unscheduled, alongside the two
    brainstorm rounds (sec 15, 16). Durable record
    docs/design/tree-mixing-proposals.md (ranking
    sec 4.1 as amended, specialization addendum sec 12).
  vartypes-attribute: door, no plan doc of its own. dbartsData's
    initialize and validateXTest honour a "varTypes" attribute on a user's
    predictor matrix, which marks columns categorical by code; nothing
    documents it, and tests use it to reach codings a factor cannot hold.
    Document it, or refuse it in favour of factor and ordered columns.
  veto-arc-doors: a per-sweep diagnostics counter, R-readable, of
    leaves that hold rows and none the likelihood sees - none exists in
    src/bartcore, so it is an additive new channel
    (docs/plans/release-candidate-review.md wave-5a landing note).
  membership-battery-entries: two mutation-battery entries for the
    membership rule, each expected killed by one gate: the moves'
    empty-leaf test reading weight (killer mask-redraw-exact.R quick
    gaussian) and setActiveRows handing the redraw a zero fit (killer
    mask-redraw-exact.R quick probit). Both were run by hand at landing
    (docs/plans/empty-leaf-membership.md).
  forest-prior-args: how a model of several forests is written, and
    what a forest's sd means. Written: each forest() is one function,
    the first included (dec-B268); a multiplier is the basis argument,
    the right-hand side of a model formula with no tilde, and the
    colon form goes (dec-B267, dec-B270); sd is a plain argument in
    the response's units, one number per forest in 1.0, with
    leaf.prior = normal(sd = ) the long form (dec-B266, dec-B269;
    dec-B246 as revised; tree.prior as dec-B246, dec-B252). Meant: a
    multiplier is a factor by its class alone (dec-B260); a numeric
    multiplier is never centred or divided, its default sd is
    sqrt(2 / K) per standard deviation of the column, derived once at
    creation and kept with the data, a stated sd is literal per unit,
    and the coefficient is normal (dec-B261, dec-B263, dec-B264; the
    sd is the forest's own, dec-B253); a replaced column keeps the
    derived default unless setForestBasis(updateBasisScale = TRUE),
    for burn-in only (dec-B265). The coefficient's law is one
    argument, amplitude: fixed() holds it and update.amplitude goes
    (dec-B271). How a size per column is stated (a named vector, a
    wrapper on the term or a unit on the term) is decided after the
    release; 1.0-0 refuses each spelling by name and fixes the
    column names and the per-column reader shapes so that any of
    them is an addition (dec-B272). A star in a basis is refused
    (dec-B273); defaults go by a forest's kind (dec-B274); extract
    returns a list by forest of vectors named by column (dec-B275).
    Landed: written-surface, forest-defaults-by-kind. Planned:
    forest-sd-unit, forest-kind-by-class, forest-multiplier. Not
    yet planned: tree.prior on forest() and the variance forest, the
    reader's normal() objects.
  written-surface-leftovers: what the basis left, none planned. A
    missing value in a basis on a row the fit keeps is refused where
    lm drops the row, a departure from "as in lm" that the help
    names. A hazard fit of several forests does not check that a
    basis covers the data under subset, its rows being expanded: on
    the matrix interface a basis covers the expanded rows, and with a
    formula it is refused for its row count. A basis carried by a
    data object, dbartsData(bases = ), keeps whatever column names
    its value has, a partly named one among them, where a value given
    to forest() is named whole or not at all; the data door is the
    kind-by-class slice's. A subset cannot be forwarded through a
    wrapper's dots when it is an expression over the data's columns,
    nor when the formula was written outside the wrapper: the data
    object's own model frame reads the reference to the dots in the
    formula's environment (the base build's too). pdbart(fit) on a
    kept fit reads its stored call's data again, so a call whose data
    draws its rows averages the curve over other rows; bartBT and the
    hazard, multinomial and hurdle paths still put values in their
    inner calls, each read once, and an error there prints them. predict(bases = ) checks no name of a column or a forest
    (forest-defaults-by-kind). setForestBasis with a factor whose
    levels are reordered keeps the old column names, and a zero
    column in a basis given to dbartsData(bases = ) is fitted
    (forest-kind-by-class). A name R or an attached
    package supplies, pi or scale, is looked up at the fit, not when
    forest() is called. A forest built by a wrapper whose formal holds the basis,
    function(b) forest(basis = b), is given the formal's value, so
    such a wrapper cannot pass on a column of the data by its name.
  control-carried-count-edges: with the control migration (dec-A179).
    Ruled: a control copied from a fit sets no count, and a warning
    says when its copy differs and is not used (dec-B334); a sampler
    assembled by new() takes the model's count with the same warning
    (dec-B335). A control taken from a bart() or bartBT() fit and given to
    dbarts() for a model whose every forest has a basis is refused as
    naming 'n.trees', though nobody named one (those functions build
    their control by a call that names it), and setControl given an
    all-basis sampler's control on another sampler is refused as
    changing the count; both were taken before defaults went by kind.
    A count written into a carried control that equals the count it
    shows cannot be told from no edit and is replaced by the carried
    one.
  forest-sd-unit: before 1.0-0 (dec-B246 as revised, dec-B253,
    dec-B266), after forest-defaults-by-kind. A forest's sd is in
    the response's units wherever it is written or read: the units
    of y, and of the latent index under probit and logistic. The
    engine divides a stated number by a unit it derives at a first
    creation (sd(y - offset) over the rows kept, 1, pi/sqrt(3)) and
    R records beside the anchor; a default does not move, and is
    reported as the number of response units it comes to. The
    engine is handed the statement, an sd or none, a hold and whether
    there is a basis, and holds the rule; the reader gives the
    statement with sd and sd.stated beside it, and extract's
    leaf.prior.sd on several forests is a list by label, the entry
    itself for one forest. A named single sd is taken on every forest
    (dec-B280, dec-B282).
    The one forest of a single-forest model takes sd as its
    leaf.prior = normal(sd = ). Engine, bridge and R. Changes the
    prior of a model that states a forest sd under a gaussian or
    logistic response and is left as written; respelled, such a
    call is equal to rounding; a fit that states none is unchanged
    to the bit. Four BCF exact gates and sbc.R are respelled, and
    bartCause's sd.moderate defaults to NULL the same day.
    Plan: docs/plans/forest-sd-unit.md.
  forest-kind-by-class: before 1.0-0 (dec-B260), after forest-sd-unit
    and before the multiplier slice. What a forest multiplies is
    decided by the class of its basis and nothing else, at every
    door: dbartsData(bases = ) takes a factor and reads a logical
    vector as levels, as forest(basis = ) does. A factor's levels
    are recorded with the data; setForestBasis and predict(bases = )
    take the class the forest was created with, a factor is matched
    to its levels by name, a forest created without a basis takes
    none, and a held forest keeps its width. A value's empty level
    or zero column is refused once, after subset. R only, two
    pushes. Moves no draw but for a logical vector through the data
    door and a factor swapped or predicted with its levels in
    another order. The second push respells the 98 blocks of
    indicator columns in the tests as the factors they stand for,
    bit for bit, and then refuses, at every door, swap and predict, a
    factor of three or more levels and a basis of several numeric
    columns (dec-B281, dec-B282); the gaussian baseline's
    three-level scenario is rewritten and re-recorded. bartCause's
    bcf passes factor(z) between the two pushes.
    Plan: docs/plans/forest-kind-by-class.md.
  forest-multiplier: before 1.0-0 (dec-B253, dec-B260 to dec-B265,
    dec-B269, dec-B271, dec-B272, dec-B275, dec-B354; dec-A171),
    after forest-kind-by-class. Each kind of forest gets one rule. A
    held coefficient is 1 and the multiplier is what the basis says,
    wherever the forest stands (1 with no basis, the column as given
    for one numeric column, 0 for a two-level factor's reference
    level and 1 for the other), the forest's sd is then exact, and no
    other shape is held; fixed() on a forest takes no value but 1,
    the refusal pointing to the basis for a coding and to sd for a
    size (dec-B354); amplitude.prior.variance goes. A numeric column
    is used as given, its coefficient normal; a stated sd is per
    unit, and the default is sqrt(2 / K) of the response's scale for
    one standard deviation of the column, taken by the engine once
    at creation and recorded on the data object; a swap moves no
    prior. A row is dropped from a forest's update by its multiplier
    relative to that forest's largest, so a column in small units
    fits as any other. print gives a block with the column's mean,
    sd and range. Three pushes: held coefficients, the numeric
    multiplier, the printed block. Changes the posterior of every
    model with a held coefficient or a numeric multiplier and of no
    other; glue_toggle of the BCF baseline is re-recorded and four
    scenarios added; one exact gate script is added, deciding by z.
    bartCause's bcf loses b.prior.variance the day the first push
    lands.
    Plan: docs/plans/forest-multiplier.md.
  factor-column-update-forms: what the joint form's fix left
    (docs/plans/factor-column-update-forms.md, landed). setPredictor by
    column and forceUpdate = "partial" read a factor given for a
    NUMERIC column through its integer codes (the column then holds 1
    to 4, no message); the joint form refuses it. The fix sits in a
    helper that whole-frame updates share. And a data frame given as
    the whole matrix fails in as.double, though the manual's x item
    speaks of a whole data-frame update. Not planned.
  install-doors-noted: measured while planning the two entries above,
    none planned and none seen to break a sampler. A DART state and an
    ordinary one install into each other, and a monotone leaf's state
    into a plain sampler of its family, though dec-B254 lists both as
    structure. Latents edited by hand to values that are not finite
    install into probit, ordinal and aft samplers. A warm start takes
    a linear or gp donor into a constant-leaf sampler, which then
    runs. benchmarks/R/composition-matrix.R stops on the tip before
    any cell runs: its header lookup finds no row for bart2() in
    docs/design/feature-matrix.md since the rename to bart, so the
    matrix gates nothing until that is fixed. A formula predictor with
    a non-syntactic name and no space, such as a column named x1:x2
    written backticked, stops in dbartsData.
  forest-after-merge: built after the merge to main, for 1.0-0 still
    (dec-B276, VD 2026-10-06): setForestBasis(updateBasisScale = TRUE),
    which re-derives a default sd from a replaced column during
    burn-in (dec-B265), and the long form leaf.prior = normal(sd = )
    on forest() (dec-B266). Until then the first is absent, a caller
    who replaces the column of a default-sd forest stating the sd,
    and the second is R's unused-argument error. Selecting a forest
    by its label is NOT deferred.
  coefficient-law-opt-in: a caller may opt a forest's drawn coefficient
    into a heavy-tailed law (half-Cauchy; half-t(3) was measured too),
    as another value of forest()'s amplitude argument, beside fixed()
    (dec-B271); the default stays normal (dec-B262). Its user is a caller who redraws
    the multiplier, whose scale drifts during a run. Wanted for 1.0-0,
    built after the merge to main (VD 2026-10-06).
  named-single-sd: forest(sd = ) takes a named single number, its name
    ignored, on every forest (dec-B280, as dec-B282 leaves it).
    Before the merge to main; a step of
    docs/plans/forest-sd-unit.md.
  factor-basis-refusal: a factor, character or logical basis of three
    or more levels is refused by name in 1.0-0, drawn or held, the
    message showing one forest for each level but the first
    (dec-B281). Before the merge to main; the second push of
    docs/plans/forest-kind-by-class.md.
  factor-basis-per-level: forest(x, basis = ~ arm) on a factor of three
    or more levels becomes shorthand for one forest for each level but
    the first, each on that level's indicator, so every level has its
    own effect function (dec-B281). After 1.0-0 (VD 2026-10-07: "make
    sure the sugar form that gets forests for all levels is scheduled
    for later"). To design: the forests' labels, where sd and
    amplitude stated on the term go, vars, and the list door.
  several-column-basis-refusal: a basis of several numeric columns is
    refused by name in 1.0-0, however written (~ dose + age, a spline,
    a matrix value), the message showing one forest for each column
    (dec-B282). Before the merge to main; the second push of
    docs/plans/forest-kind-by-class.md. bartCause's bcf hands
    cbind(1 - z, z) through dbartsData(bases = ) and moves to
    factor(z) first.
  forests-shared-total: a model may hold several forests with no
    basis, y ~ forest(x1) + forest(x2) + forest(x3), which share one
    prior total, each an equal part, as the trees of a forest do; a
    forest with a basis keeps its own prior (dec-B292, dec-B291).
    After the merge to main and before 1.0-0 (VD 2026-10-07:
    "Post-merge, pre-1.0"); until then several such forests stay
    refused. To design: how the engine tells them apart (today by
    their multipliers; the workaround is a basis column of ones),
    whose the fitting function's tree count and tree prior are
    (dec-B274), a stated sd on one of them, and probit and logistic.
    Until then a constant basis column with sd stated is created, the
    one way to write such forests (dec-B307); decide with this item
    whether it stays a second spelling.
  several-column-basis-meaning: what a basis of several numeric
    columns means, shorthand for a forest each or one shared shape
    times a weighted sum (the curve-times-modifier model of a spline
    basis is the second), with how a size is stated for each column
    (dec-B272). After 1.0-0 (dec-B282).
  held-coefficients-in-state: a state from a sampler that draws a
    two-level factor's coefficients installs into one that holds them,
    in silence: the trees arrive, the coefficients stay 0 and 1, and
    the install reports itself exact (test-state-not-model.R pins the
    rule as "fixed amplitudes"; critique of forest-multiplier,
    section 10). After the multiplier slice's held push it is the one
    place left where a sampler runs trees drawn under other held
    values. Not planned.
  response-scale-rows: what a fit takes from the response to place or
    size a prior (the count fit's centre, the gaussian and Student-t
    response scale, the survival fit's, the unit of a forest's sd) is
    computed over the rows in the likelihood when the sampler is
    created, rows at weight 0 or masked out left aside; it is held
    when weights or the mask change, and setWeights and setActiveRows
    take the response setters' argument to re-derive it, with the
    response setters' effect on the live chain (dec-B296, revising
    dec-A140; dec-B327). A sampler created with no row in takes them
    over every row, with a warning (dec-B302); a re-derivation whose
    rows in hold fewer than two distinct values keeps the prior in
    force and warns once per sampler (dec-B364). A linear or gp leaf's
    covariate centre and scale follow the same rule (dec-B304), and a
    multiplier's scale does in the multiplier plan (dec-B303).
    A sampler made by hand is created with every row in; with slice C,
    dbartsSpec's help gives setActiveRows(spec$active, updateScale =
    TRUE) as the call that makes it dbarts()'s, and the loop form, in
    place of the plan's setOffset call (dec-B363).
    Cut points still span every row. Changes the
    draws of every fit created with rows at weight 0. Before 1.0-0,
    and before the merge if it can be: design first, with the
    state-frame slices that give derived data its record and with
    docs/plans/forest-sd-unit.md, which states the unit.
    Planned as four slices (dec-A176): the survival re-anchor from
    observed times, landed 2026-10-07; creation
    only, docs/plans/response-scale-rows.md; then the two new
    arguments and dec-B304, stated at that plan's end and not planned.
    Fewer than two distinct response values in take the fallback too
    (dec-B309).
  state-store-size-install: setState installs a state whose store of
    kept trees is larger or smaller than the sampler's: the live chain
    as from any state, the most recent kept draws that fit, the
    sampler's n.samples unchanged, and TRUE, the store being output
    and not model (dec-B318), where it refuses today with "state is not
    consistent with this sampler" (dec-B294, revising dec-A138 in
    part). Before the merge to main; the kept-draw store in the
    engine and the state's reader, after leaf-conversions, which
    works in the same store.
  bartcause-thread-default: on bartCause's dbarts-1.0 branch, bcf.R
    computes n.threads from dbarts::guessNumCores() and passes it as
    stated, so where the cores cannot be counted bcf() is refused;
    leave n.threads out of the call to dbarts when the guess is NA.
  missingness-first-seen: a column can be missing from the first
    missing value it is given, at creation or by setPredictor, and
    ever after (dec-B321; setData not ruled): the engine's flag no longer
    follows the column's content, the first missing value draws each
    existing split's direction with probability one half where today
    it sends them left, and no direction is dropped afterwards
    (dec-B320). predict and test data keep refusing a missing value
    in a column that could not be missing (dec-B322). Before the merge
    to main; with the install surface's first slice, which keeps
    directions at setState.
  copy-merge-weight-order: after a forced setCutPoints or refresh that
    merges, a copy's merged leaf can differ from its original's by one
    unit in the last place when the leaf's weights do not sum exactly
    (runif weights: 1 run in 10 on arm64, 3 on x86), the merge summing
    weights in each sampler's own row order; exact weights agree.
  refused-update-rounding: after a refused whole-matrix setPredictor
    the next draws differ from an untouched twin's by about 1.8e-15,
    where the help says the fits are as they were.
  forced-update-returns-null: the forced forms of setState and
    installTrees return NULL invisibly when they are built, as a forced
    setPredictor does (dec-B310); the unforced value says whether a
    valid update was made. Before the merge to main.
  setstate-force-update: setState takes forceUpdate, FALSE by
    default, in setPredictor's idiom: unforced it installs a state
    that goes in cleanly and returns TRUE, or leaves the sampler
    untouched and returns FALSE; forced it installs, changing what it
    must (dec-B305, revising dec-B234 and dec-A167). A conversion
    between response units and a kept store of another size are
    clean (dec-B318), and so is a split keeping its direction for
    missing values on a column with none now (dec-B320); a tree that
    must change to fit is not; copy()
    and a reload force. Before the merge to main. Design first, with warm-start-salvage and
    state-store-size-install, which are the same surface: what the
    forced form returns (ruled: NULL, dec-B310), one name with
    installTrees's force argument,
    the package's and stan4bart's callers.
  precision-floor-edge-values: the floor that refuses scales and
    Polya-Gamma variates that are not positive and finite passes a
    value at the edge of what a double holds: a variate of 1e-320 in a
    logistic or negative-binomial block installs and the next sweep
    does not return, and the largest finite double as a scale gives
    fits that are not finite. No stored state holds such a value; a
    block edited by hand can, within one family as across two
    (docs/plans/state-family-record-removal.md, landing note).
  single-forest-vars-leftovers: two things the column restriction on a
    single declared forest left (docs/design/single-forest-vars.md). A
    zero split probability does not exclude a column once the positive
    ones run out along a path, with split.probs alone and no vars: with
    two 0/1 columns held to one cut each (n.cuts of 1, or quantile cuts)
    beside a column given probability zero, that column is split on
    about 45000 times in 2000 sweeps of 20 trees, in nearly every sweep.
    On the default grid of 100 cuts it is rare, not absent: none on
    five fixtures of six and about 3500 such splits on the sixth.
    setmodel-parameters-not-structure carries the fix. And the model's
    forest.columns attribute moves into the first forest's record when
    the control migration gives every model one.
  weighted-binary: arbitrary real weights on the binary families need
    a real-shape Polya-Gamma sampler; IPW is a pseudo-likelihood trap,
    and the approximate-PG decision is shared with
    negbin-real-dispersion. Integer-weight logistic ships via
    replicated latents, and probit refuses weights outright - no
    tractable weighted latent form. docs/plans/weighted-binary.md.
    decision-gated, scheduled after 1.0-0 (VD 2026-08-28).

absolute residual prior scale (decided, VD 2026-09-14)
  post-release, R only: chisq gains an absolute spelling,
  chisq(df, scale = lambda), which names the prior literally instead of
  through the sigest its quantile calibrates against, and ignores
  sigest as fixed() does. Additive - a new argument beside quant, no
  spelling retired, so no tombstone. The residual prior's one home on
  the family object is the surface it is added to (docs/decisions.md
  dec-B123). scheduled after 1.0-0 (VD 2026-09-14).

